#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "Improving energy estimation of a Fermionic lattice model with SQD"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<OpenInLabBanner notebookPath=\"qiskit-"
"addons/sqd/02_fermionic_lattice_hamiltonian.ipynb\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In this tutorial we implement a [Qiskit "
"pattern](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/guides/intro-to-patterns) "
"showing how to post-process noisy quantum samples to find an approximation "
"to the ground state of a Fermionic lattice Hamiltonian known as the single-"
"impurity Anderson model. We will follow a sample-based quantum "
"diagonalization approach to process samples taken from a set of ``16``-qubit"
" Krylov basis states over increasing time intervals. These states are "
"realized on the quantum device using Trotterization of the time evolution. "
"In order to account for the effect of quantum noise, the configuration "
"recovery technique is used. Assuming a good initial state and sparsity of "
"the ground state, [this approach is proven to converge "
"efficiently](https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.09702).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The pattern can be described in four steps:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Step 1: Map to quantum problem**\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '    - '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Generate a set of Krylov basis states (i.e., Trotterized time-evolution "
"circuits) over increasing time intervals for estimating the ground state\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Step 2: Optimize the problem**\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '    - '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Transpile the circuits for the backend\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Step 3: Execute experiments**\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '    - '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Draw samples from the circuits using the ``Sampler`` primitive\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '4. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Step 4: Post-process results**\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '   - '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Self-consistent configuration recovery loop\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '       - '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Post-process the full set of bitstring samples, using prior knowledge of "
"particle number and the average orbital occupancy calculated on the most "
"recent iteration\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '       - '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Probabilistically create batches of subsamples from recovered bitstrings\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '       - '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Project and diagonalize the Fermionic lattice Hamiltonian over each sampled "
"subspace\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '       - '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Save the minimum ground state energy found across all batches and update the"
" avg orbital occupancy\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 1: Map problem to a quantum circuit {#step-1-map-problem-to-a-quantum-"
"circuit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"First, we will create the one- and two-body Hamiltonians describing the one-"
"dimensional single-impurity Anderson model (SIAM) with ``7`` bath sites "
"(``8`` electrons in ``8`` orbitals). This model is used to describe magnetic"
" impurities embedded in metals.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Then we will create the ``16``-qubit Trotter circuits used to generate the "
"quantum Krylov subspace.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next, we will generate the quantum Krylov subspace with a set of Trotterized"
" quantum circuits. Here we create helpers for generating the initial "
"(reference) state as well as the time evolution for the one- and two-body "
"parts of the Hamiltonian. For a more detailed description of this model and "
"how the circuits are designed, please refer to [the "
"paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.09702).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Generate ``d`` time-evolved states that specify the quantum Krylov subspace."
" Here, we have chosen ``d=8``. The error from sampling Krylov basis states "
"converges with increasing ``d``. Note that the particulars of this problem "
"instance allow for an efficient compilation of the one-body evolution using "
"`OrbitalRotationJW`; however, in general, one could use Qiskit's "
"[PauliEvolutionGate](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/api/qiskit/qiskit.circuit.library.PauliEvolutionGate)"
" to implement the Trotterized time evolution of the full Hamiltonian.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Quantum circuit diagram](/img/qiskit-addons/sqd/02-fermionic-lattice-"
"hamiltonian/output_1.png)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Quantum circuit diagram](/img/qiskit-addons/sqd/02-fermionic-lattice-"
"hamiltonian/output_2.png)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Step 2: Optimize the problem {#step-2-optimize-the-problem}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"After we have created the Trotterized circuits, we will optimize them for a "
"target hardware. We need to choose the hardware device to use before "
"optimization. We will use a fake 127-qubit backend from "
"``qiskit_ibm_runtime`` to emulate a real device. To run on a real QPU, just "
"replace the fake backend with a real backend. Check out the [Qiskit IBM "
"Runtime docs](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/guides/get-started-with-"
"primitives#get-started-with-sampler) for more info.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next, we will transpile the circuits to the target backend using Qiskit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Step 3: Execute experiments {#step-3-execute-experiments}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"After optimizing the circuits for hardware execution, we are ready to run "
"them on the target hardware and collect samples for ground state energy "
"estimation. Here we use ``SamplerV2`` from ``qiskit-ibm-runtime`` to "
"simulate noisy samples taken from the ``ibm_sherbrooke`` backend. We then "
"combine the counts from each of the Krylov basis states into a single counts"
" dictionary and plot the top 20 most commonly sampled bitstrings.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"***Note: [Qiskit Aer](https://qiskit.github.io/qiskit-aer/index.html) is "
"required to simulate samples from transpiled circuits.***\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Quantum circuit diagram](/img/qiskit-addons/sqd/02-fermionic-lattice-"
"hamiltonian/output_3.png)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Step 4: Post-process the results {#step-4-post-process-the-results}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now, we run the SQD algorithm using the `diagonalize_fermionic_hamiltonian` "
"function. See the [API "
"documentation](../apidocs/qiskit_addon_sqd.fermion.rst#qiskit_addon_sqd.fermion.diagonalize_fermionic_hamiltonian)"
" for explanations of the arguments to this function.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now, we plot the results. The first plot shows that after a few iterations, "
"we obtain the exact ground state energy.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This example is small enough that we are able to explore the full Hilbert "
"space, as seen in the print statements above. Remember, the full Hilbert "
"space is dimension ``(num_orbitals choose nelec_a) * (num_orbitals choose "
"nelec_b)``. So for this problem: `(8 choose 4)**2 = 4900`. The subspace "
"dimensions increase due enhanced configuration recovery, and also the fact "
"that the SQD algorithm carries important configurations from one iteration "
"to the next. By the last iteration, we are diagonalizing in the entire "
"Hilbert space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The second plot shows the average occupancy of each spatial orbital across "
"all batches' solutions. We see that with high probability each orbital "
"contains one electron.\n"
msgstr ""

